<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <style name="Theme.Search.Splash" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splashBg</item>
        <!--
          No icon background.

          windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor paints a shape behind the icon,
          for icons that would otherwise have no contrast against the splash.
          The owl is light on dark and the splash is dark, so it never needed
          one - and keeping it meant keeping two colours in step. They fell out
          of step: values-night overrides splashBg and not splashIconBg, so in
          dark mode the patch stayed #220549 while the splash moved to #2A1E48
          and the shape became visible. Light mode hid it only because the two
          values happened to be equal.

          Removing it deletes the whole class of bug rather than adding a night
          variant that has to be remembered next time either colour changes.
        -->
        <!--
          No windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon, deliberately.

          Left unset, the platform puts the app's LAUNCHER icon on the splash -
          which is the point. One UI opens an app by growing the launcher icon
          into the window, and that icon is the adaptive one: gradient,
          squircle mask, owl. Supplying a separate splash drawable meant the
          launcher grew a squircle and then the splash replaced it with a bare
          owl on flat purple, so a single launch showed two different marks.

          Now the thing that expands and the thing that lands are the same
          object, and the handover is invisible.

          It also removes a whole file that had to be kept in sync by hand: the
          splash owl was sized at 83% of its canvas while the icon foreground
          is at 66%, and nothing but memory connected those numbers.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
    </style>
</resources>
